  "notes": "The fastest entry in this batch by a clear margin — 96–108 where the lane's norm is 90–100 — and the only one whose build order is documented backwards. He finishes everything else before the drums: \"that's how I figure out where to put the kick and the snare.\" That is modelled as a kit that answers a busy bass: the highest `kick.densityPerBar`, `syncopation` and `offbeat8thShare` on this axis, five percussion lanes, and a bass with the lowest `followRootsProb` and the highest `anticipationProb` here because the research calls it the leading voice of the groove, highly syncopated with rests. The harmony is real P-Funk played on keys — dominant-7 and 9th vamps over `mixolydian`, with the case-corrected `IV` and `VII` families that a funk vamp actually uses, and Rhodes rather than the parent's portamento worm on top. Full song forms with bridge and breakdown come from his own account, so the arrangement authors a bridge and a long vamp outro. ⚠ `modes` are authored because the parent's Death Row moods pin 90–100 and would have made his own tempo unreachable. ⚠ `medium` per the research's high-medium: the build order and the layer order are quoted, the tempo band and the chord families are read from the catalogue. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 48 to 45 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 46 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3.",
